(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 42-year-old treated for a corneal abrasion three days ago returns with worsening pain and a white spot on the cornea. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A rust ring has formed spontaneously","A hyphema has developed","A corneal ulcer has developed","The abrasion is healing normally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is why abrasions are re-examined, and why anaesthetic drops are never sent home. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 8-year-old struck by a ball has severe pain, vomiting and a heart rate of 48 whenever he tries to look up. His eye and lids look normal. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Migraine triggered by the injury","A white-eyed blowout","Simple concussion","Vasovagal syncope from pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In children entrapment can occur with no orbital soft tissue signs at all. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why does the bruising in an orbital contusion stay in front of the eye rather than spreading behind it?","choices":["The periosteum is impermeable","Venous drainage carries it forward","The septum acts as a wall","The orbital fat absorbs the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is what keeps it preseptal. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 19-year-old arrives with a fish hook embedded through the cornea. He is stable and in pain. Which is the most appropriate management of the hook?","choices":["Leave it in place and arrange surgical repair","Withdraw it along its entry path","Cut it flush with the cornea and cover it","Remove it after topical anaesthetic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removing it can extrude intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 41-year-old welder felt something strike his eye while grinding without goggles. Vision is 20/40 and the eye is quiet. Which imaging is appropriate, and which must be avoided?","choices":["Computed tomography of the orbit; avoid magnetic resonance imaging","Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbit; avoid computed tomography","Ocular ultrasound with firm probe pressure; avoid computed tomography","No imaging, since acuity is preserved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A magnetic field can move a metallic fragment through the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with long-standing proliferative diabetic retinopathy has a localised, concave retinal detachment. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the underlying systemic disease alone","Observation with serial imaging","Intravitreal antibiotics","Surgical repair"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A traction detachment is managed surgically. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 26-year-old with hyphema has an intraocular pressure of 34 mmHg (normal 10 to 21) and no suspicion of an open globe. Which agents are used?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids alone","Aspirin to improve perfusion","A mydriatic to open the angle","Beta blockers and acetazolamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is how a raised pressure is brought down here. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 37-year-old has crepitus in the eyelid after blowing his nose following facial trauma. Which finding is this?","choices":["Subcutaneous emphysema","Orbital cellulitis","Chemosis","Enophthalmos"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Air tracks from the sinus into the orbit through the fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 40-year-old is suspected of an orbital fracture after a blow. Which imaging is ordered?","choices":["Plain facial radiographs alone","Ocular ultrasound","No imaging unless diplopia is present","Computed tomography of the orbits and midface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the study for this fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 35-year-old has periorbital swelling and ecchymosis after a fall, with a normal globe and no haemorrhage within the orbit. Beyond supportive care, which is the most appropriate step?","choices":["Start topical antibiotics","Arrange urgent canthotomy","Refer for cataract assessment","Exclude brain trauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The force that bruised the orbit reaches the brain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old with an eye injury is intoxicated and gives an inconsistent account. Which is the most appropriate way to establish the mechanism?","choices":["Accept the account as given","Record the mechanism as unknown and proceed","Ask family, friends or bystanders","Wait until he is sober to take a history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The circumstance of injury still has to be established. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 22-year-old with a traumatic hyphema is admitted. Which regimen fits, and what is it aiming at?","choices":["Topical anaesthetic and discharge, with review in a week","Bed rest head-up and aminocaproic acid, to prevent a rebleed","Ambulation and warm compresses, to clear the blood faster","Aspirin, to prevent clot organisation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every element serves that one goal. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 16-year-old was hit in the eye by a paintball. There is a layer of blood across the lower anterior chamber with a visible fluid level. Which is the most likely diagnosis, and what else must be considered?","choices":["Traumatic iritis with cells in the chamber","Hyphema, and it can itself indicate an open globe","Subconjunctival haemorrhage overlying the iris","Vitreous haemorrhage seen through the pupil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is not always an isolated injury. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A metallic corneal foreign body is removed from a 45-year-old, leaving a brown ring in the stroma. Which is the most appropriate management of that ring?","choices":["Wipe it with a cotton-tipped applicator","Leave it, since it will resorb","Remove it with a battery-operated burr","Irrigate repeatedly with saline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A rust ring from iron or copper comes out with a burr. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has double vision on looking to the side after blunt orbital trauma. Which muscle is entrapped?","choices":["Inferior oblique","Medial rectus","Inferior rectus","Superior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lateral gaze diplopia points to the medial rectus and a medial wall fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with hypertension and a known intraocular tumour has subretinal fluid but no retinal break on dilated examination. Which type of detachment is this?","choices":["Posterior vitreous detachment","Exudative","Traction","Combined rhegmatogenous and traction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No break and no traction, with an associated tumour. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 21-year-old has a full-thickness upper lid laceration. Beyond repairing the lid, which is the most important step?","choices":["Patch the eye for 48 hours","Prescribe a cycloplegic","Examine the globe underneath","Start oral corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. About two thirds of full-thickness lid lacerations come with one. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 23-year-old has a laceration of the upper lid and a subconjunctival haemorrhage after being struck with a bottle. He is alert. Which is the most appropriate next step before any lid repair?","choices":["Measure the intraocular pressure first","Assess the globe beneath","Close the lid and arrange follow-up","Instil a mydriatic to see the retina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two thirds of full-thickness lid lacerations have a globe injury with them. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which bones may be involved in a basilar skull fracture?","choices":["The nasal bones and the vomer","The parietal bones only","Ethmoid, frontal, temporal, sphenoid or occipital","The maxilla and the zygomatic arch alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those make up the skull base. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 58-year-old myope describes a shower of floaters, flashes of light, and a curtain moving across the vision of one eye. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal vein occlusion","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Exudative retinal detachment","Traction retinal detachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flashes, floaters and a curtain, with myopia as the risk factor. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 52-year-old has subretinal fluid with no retinal break and no traction, alongside a known intraocular tumour. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the underlying condition","Scleral buckling","Pneumatic retinopexy","Observation with no treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An exudative detachment is not primarily surgical. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Computed tomography confirms an orbital floor fracture with inferior rectus entrapment in a 30-year-old. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Contact a facial trauma surgeon urgently","Ice, analgesia and review in 2 to 3 days","Admit for neurosurgical observation","Start antibiotics and discharge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An entrapped muscle can necrose if its blood supply is compromised. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 31-year-old has a corneal abrasion from a fingernail. Which treatment is appropriate?","choices":["A topical corticosteroid","No treatment and no follow-up","A topical antibacterial","A topical antiviral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and patching may ease the pain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 33-year-old has a 1 centimetre laceration running through the lower lid margin after a dog bite. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Close it in the emergency department with fine sutures","Leave it open and review in one week","Refer to plastic surgery as an outpatient","Ophthalmology, because the lid margin is involved"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Margin involvement is one of the explicit referral criteria. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 72-year-old with previous cataract surgery in both eyes is struck in the face by a car airbag. Why does the surgical history matter here?","choices":["Old incisions leave a permanent weak point","It makes a hyphema more likely than a rupture","It protects the eye from blunt force","It rules out an open globe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prior intraocular surgery lowers the force needed. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old on warfarin fell and struck his face, and is now confused. His right eye is bruised. Which is the most appropriate imaging decision?","choices":["No imaging unless vision is reduced","Magnetic resonance imaging of the head","Plain skull radiographs","Computed tomography without contrast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both are on the list that makes imaging automatic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which detail about the mechanism most changes the level of concern in ocular trauma?","choices":["Blunt or sharp, high or low velocity","Whether the patient was wearing spectacles","The time of day the injury happened","The patient's occupation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is what predicts the injury pattern. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of these lid lacerations can reasonably be repaired in the emergency department?","choices":["A superficial skin cut sparing the margin","A cut 4 millimetres from the medial canthus","A cut associated with new ptosis","A cut through the tarsal plate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Partial-thickness lacerations meeting none of the criteria are repaired there. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 60-year-old on warfarin develops a tense, proptotic orbit with rising pressure and no history of injury. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Pressure patching the eye","Topical beta blocker alone","Immediate enucleation","Canthotomy with cantholysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Releasing the lateral canthal tendon and cutting its inferior branch lets the blood drain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 20-year-old with a hyphema is being discharged after admission. Which instruction matters most?","choices":["Stop the cycloplegic once the pain settles","Avoid aspirin, antiplatelets and straining","Use topical anaesthetic drops when the eye aches","Resume contact sport as soon as the blood clears"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both raise the risk of a secondary haemorrhage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
